Title:
Function Stomping Injection (Local & Remote)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This write-up explains “function stomping” as a shellcode injection technique that overwrites the bytes of an existing exported DLL function with a payload instead of allocating new executable memory.  
- It aims to reduce suspicious indicators associated with classic injection patterns that rely on `VirtualAlloc/VirtualAllocEx` for RWX/RX regions.  
- The post covers how to select a low-impact “sacrificial” function (example: `SetupScanFileQueueA` in `setupapi.dll`) to avoid crashing the process due to stomping a frequently used API.  
- It provides step-by-step C/WinAPI implementations for both local stomping (within the same process) and remote stomping (writing into another process).  
- Remote stomping includes a prerequisite DLL-load step (DLL injection via `LoadLibraryA` in a remote thread) so the target function exists in the victim process before overwriting it.  
- Useful for malware developers, red teamers, and pentesters studying alternative code injection tradecraft and its operational constraints.

Technical Focus:
- Function stomping (overwriting exported DLL function code)
- Memory protection changes (`VirtualProtect`, `VirtualProtectEx`)
- Remote memory write and execution (`WriteProcessMemory`, `CreateRemoteThread`)
- DLL injection to ensure module presence (`VirtualAllocEx` + `LoadLibraryA`)
- Process enumeration and handle acquisition (`EnumProcesses`, `OpenProcess`, `EnumProcessModules`)
- Sacrificial DLL/function selection and stability considerations

Use Cases:
- Execute shellcode without creating a new allocation for payload storage (reuse existing executable pages)
- Remote process code execution by overwriting an exported function in a loaded module
- Tradecraft experimentation for injection techniques that may evade simplistic “new RX/RWX allocation” heuristics
- Building PoCs to test EDR detections around module tampering and code integrity

Keywords:
function stomping, code injection, shellcode, Windows, WinAPI, LoadLibraryA, GetProcAddress, VirtualProtect, VirtualProtectEx, WriteProcessMemory, CreateThread, CreateRemoteThread, VirtualAllocEx, EnumProcesses, OpenProcess, setupapi.dll, SetupScanFileQueueA, DLL injection, module mapping, memory protections